{"arm": "X2-F", "compounds": 0, "compounds_kept": 0, "diagnoses": [{"cell": 12, "cost_per_unit": "Per lexed token: an ordered walk over the compiled rules, one consume call (an islice and a deque built to skip characters), one isinstance test, and for word tokens up to nine keyword-dictionary probes. Per grouped token: about 12 imt calls, 17 match calls and 17 flatten resumptions, the last becau", "deal_round": 2, "doubling": "Doubling the number of statements doubles the time, since statements are independent and of fixed size. Doubling a statement's length or nesting grows the value rebuild faster than linearly: leaves times nesting depth, and quadratic in list length where identifier lists are extended one comma at a t", "gap": "A statement's text is lexed twice in a row with identical results (format, then parse). A leaf's text is re-yielded once per enclosing group creation and per nesting level, instead of being read once from its parent's children. The lexer also redoes configuration-only work per token (the skip iterat", "least_work": "Lex each distinct text once, with one rule match and one keyword probe per token. Build each group's text from its direct children, whose text is already stored. Visit a token once per grouping pass that can actually match it.", "library": "sqlparse", "run": 1, "unit_count": "get_tokens is resumed 66,078 times across three lexings of the same SQL (whole script for split, each of the 160 statements for format and again for parse), so about 22k tokens per lexing. Grouping runs twice per statement (about 44k token-groupings), and these produce 524,370 imt calls, 751,442 mat", "unit_of_work": "One token: a (ttype, value) pair produced by Lexer.get_tokens, wrapped as a sql.Token by the splitter, then visited as a tree node by each of the roughly 25 grouping passes and by the filters."}], "experiment": "ideas-x2-round", "kept": 0, "lane_views": [{"lane": "obvious", "served_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "view_sha": "b4b1db61"}, {"lane